Data Normalization is the process of structuring a relational database to reduce data redundancy and improve data integrity. 
It's essentially a set of rules—called 'Normal Forms'—that dictate how we divide large, messy tables into smaller,
linked tables to ensure that every piece of information is stored in exactly one place.

Technical Breakdown :-

First Normal Form (1NF): The rule of atomicity. Every column must contain a single, indivisible value, and each record must be unique.
If a 'Customer' table has a 'Phone_Numbers' column containing 555-1234, 555-9876 in a single cell, it violates 1NF. We must split those into separate rows.

Second Normal Form (2NF): The rule of full dependency. The table must be in 1NF, and all non-key columns must depend on the entire primary key.
This usually applies to tables with composite keys. For example, in an Order_Details table with a composite key of (OrderID, ProductID),
having a ProductName column violates 2NF because ProductName only depends on ProductID, not OrderID.
We extract the product details into a separate Products table.

Third Normal Form (3NF): The rule of no transitive dependency. The table must be in 2NF, and non-key columns cannot depend on other non-key columns.
If a Customer table has ZipCode and City, City technically depends on ZipCode, not the Customer ID.
To achieve 3NF, we move ZipCode and City into a separate Locations table.



Buisness Context :- 
The business value of normalization is preventing update anomalies.
If a company changes its name from 'Apple' to 'Apple Inc.', and that data isn't normalized (it's repeated a million times across sales records),
you have to update a million rows. If it's in 3NF, you update exactly one row in the Company table, and the change instantly cascades everywhere.


Design Decision :- 
While 3NF is the absolute holy grail for operational databases (OLTP) where write-speed and consistency are paramount,
as a data engineer building analytical data warehouses (OLAP),
I actually de-normalize data intentionally. If I'm building a Kimball Star Schema in Snowflake,
I'll deliberately violate 3NF and merge that Locations table right back into my Dim_Customer table.
Storage is cheap today, but JOIN operations on petabyte-scale data are incredibly expensive. Denormalizing speeds up our BI dashboards exponentially.
